    {
      "abstract": "Abstract The mooring system plays a critical role in controlling platform motion and load transfer in floating offshore wind turbines (FOWTs), particularly in deep-water applications. In this study, the dynamic responses of a 15 MW semi-submersible FOWT with different polyester segment ratios in a chain–polyester–chain (CPC) mooring system are investigated using OpenFAST. A baseline configuration with a 265 m polyester segment is defined as 100%, and additional cases with 70% and 40% polyester lengths are considered. The dynamic responses, including platform surge motion, fairlead tension, and anchor vertical load, are systematically analyzed under identical environmental conditions. The results show that when the polyester segment is relatively short, the mooring system behavior remains dominated by the chain segments, and the surge response is similar to that of the conventional catenary mooring system. As the polyester segment length increases, the surge motion and fairlead tension are significantly reduced due to the transition from a slack catenary configuration to a more efficient load-bearing structure. The vertical anchor load exhibits a non-monotonic trend, increasing at low polyester ratios due to larger line inclination and decreasing at higher ratios as a result of reduced platform motion and overall tension. Furthermore, a clear positive correlation is observed between surge motion and mooring force, indicating that mooring loads are primarily governed by platform displacement rather than solely by mooring configuration. The results demonstrate that an appropriate polyester segment ratio can effectively balance platform motion and mooring loads, providing useful guidance for engineering design and optimization of composite mooring systems.",
