    {
      "abstract": "Abstract With the rapid growth of deep-water offshore wind power, Spar floating wind turbines have become a key foundation type due to their high stability, low motions, and deep-water adaptability. The mooring system, as the sole external constraint, strongly influences the platform’s dynamic response, especially under mooring failures. Spar FOWTs typically use a Delta mooring configuration to enhance yaw-restoring capacity, yet many studies neglect or simplify it, causing deviations in predicted post-failure responses. This study develops a fully coupled aero-hydro-servo-elastic-mooring dynamic model of a Spar FOWT with Delta mooring using the FAST-to-AQWA (F2A) real-time coupling framework. The model systematically investigates dynamic responses and secondary load redistribution following mooring branch failures under complex sea states. Results show marked differences between design and extreme conditions, while the Delta mooring provides sufficient redundancy and buffering to withstand transient loads. The findings offer critical references for safety assessment and reliability design of deep-water floating wind turbine mooring systems.",
